/* client_architecture 11: "Layout is safe-area driven with no fixed pixel
   positions." Nothing here is positioned at a screen coordinate. The margins
   below are GUTTERS inside a root that WaveRunner pads with SafeAreaBinder -
   the same binder BootController applies to the shell.

   VERTICALLY ONLY. SafeAreaBinder sets paddingTop and paddingBottom and
   NOTHING ELSE, so margin-top does measure from the safe area's edge and
   margin-left still measures from the DISPLAY's. An earlier version of this
   comment claimed both. Latent while the project stays portrait-locked, where
   the horizontal inset is zero; the first landscape build on a notched device
   is where it stops being latent.

   The bars ARE absolutely positioned, because they track world bodies; that
   position comes from the camera and is clamped into the frame, never taken
   from a constant.

   THIS IS THE ONE SCREEN THAT IS NOT PAPER-WHITE, and that is deliberate
   rather than an oversight in the design pass. It renders OVER the wave
   scene, not over the shell, so `--paper` here would be a white sheet across
   the battlefield. Light text on the world is correct; what moves onto the
   token palette is the semantic colour of the bars, so a chilled raider is
   the same teal as Vetch everywhere else. bible 10.1 asks for the interface
   to be recessive and the creatures to carry the colour - over live combat
   that argument points this way.

   AND THIS IS WHY IT TAKES NO ScreenScaffold. It is an overlay, not a screen:
   WaveRunner adds it to the wave scene's own `document.rootVisualElement`
   and it never reaches ScreenHost, never enters the back stack, and has no
   header, no CTA row and nothing to scroll. ScaffoldTests exempts it BY NAME
   for that reason, and the exemption is asserted to be exactly two names, so
   giving this file a scaffold to satisfy a sweep - or moving the type out of
   Broodline.UI.Screens to dodge one - would both be working around the test
   rather than with it.

   WHAT PHASE 8 TASK 11 CHANGED, since "no scaffold" is not "no work". Three
   raw colours left this file and the last two bespoke surfaces on it became
   the vocabulary the rest of the app uses:
     - rgba(46, 42, 61, 0.55) on the track is --hud-scrim, which IS --ink-deep
       at 55% and is now a token because the readout wants the same fill. It
       was a raw rgba() sitting in the one file verify-uss-tokens.sh's own
       header names as the reason that gate exists - and rgba() is not hex, so
       check 1 never saw it.
     - rgb(250, 249, 252) and rgb(240, 238, 246), the readout's ink and the
       tag's, are one --surface. They were six levels apart, which is not a
       hierarchy anybody reads; over a scene that changes every frame both
       want the most contrast available, and one ink is one fewer pair to keep
       in step.
     - the readout is a chip rather than bare text, and each bar is wrapped in
       `.elev-1`. Both are the same argument: on --paper a surface can be told
       apart from its background by a hairline, and over a battlefield it
       cannot.

   THIS IS THE ONE STYLESHEET IN TASKS 9-12 THAT GREW - 28 declarations to 32
   - AND THE REASON IS NOT THAT LAYOUT WAS RE-DERIVED. Step 5's rule is a test
   for re-derivation and every one of the five added declarations describes a
   SURFACE THAT DID NOT EXIST: four are the readout chip (align-self, padding,
   fill, radius) written in Theme.uss's own `.panel-*` recipe rather than a
   fourth shape, and one is the elevation wrapper's cancelled top padding. One
   declaration left (the tag's font-size, which `t-num` now carries). Nothing
   here duplicates a class the layer already provides, and the three other
   sheets this task touched went 23 to 9 and 31 to 10.

   THERE IS NO WAVE NUMBER HERE, THOUGH THE HANDOFF AND TASK 11 BOTH ASK FOR
   ONE. The handoff's Wave Defense layout opens "Wave counter and Ark health",
   and `HudSnapshot` carries `Integrity` and `Tick` and nothing else -
   Model/WaveReport.cs, three fields. Adding it is a change to
   Broodline.Model, to WaveRunner's snapshot builder and to
   `WaveHudScreen.Integrity`, whose sentence DeviceReplayTests' re-capture
   procedure reads off the screen to time a tap. That is a data-path change
   and this is a look pass, so the counter is recorded as owed rather than
   invented from whatever `WaveRunner` happens to have in scope. */
.wave-hud-view {
    flex-grow: 1;
    flex-direction: column;
}

/* THE READOUT IS A CHIP, IN Theme.uss's `.panel-*` RECIPE AND NOT A FOURTH
 * SHAPE - fill, --radius-row, one padding step - because the five panel
 * classes are what a tinted surface looks like everywhere else in the app,
 * and a HUD that invented its own would be the bespoke styling this task is
 * here to retire. It is not one of those classes only because none of them is
 * scrim: every `.panel-*` is a light tint for ink on paper, and this one
 * carries white ink over a battlefield. --space-2 rather than --space-3
 * because a readout is a chip and a panel is a block; both are on the scale.
 *
 * `align-self` IS THE HALF THAT MAKES IT A CHIP RATHER THAN A BAND. The root
 * is a column, so a child stretches the full width by default - and a
 * full-width fill under one short line is a strip across the top of the
 * battlefield, not a readout. `flex-start` shrink-wraps it to its own text,
 * which is also what keeps the band `ClampIntoFrame` reserves the width of
 * the thing actually being avoided. */
.wave-hud-view__integrity {
    align-self: flex-start;
    margin-left: var(--gutter);
    margin-top: var(--space-2);
    padding: var(--space-2);
    background-color: var(--hud-scrim);
    border-radius: var(--radius-row);
    color: var(--surface);
}

/* `t-num` is on the Label in the UXML and its size is overridden HERE, on a
 * two-class selector, which is the one override in this file and is stated
 * rather than left to the cascade.
 *
 * bible 10.6's 11px is a FLOOR, not a target, and --text-secondary sits
 * exactly on it. This is the single number a player reads mid-wave, at arm's
 * length, with a thumb somewhere over the screen, against a scene that is
 * moving - so it takes the largest size on the scale that still reads as
 * chrome rather than as a headline. It was a bespoke 15px before this task,
 * which is on no scale at all; 17px is --text-cta, the project's own "read
 * this at a glance" size, and it is the nearer of the two tokens either side
 * of 15 in the direction that does not shrink the readout. */
.wave-hud-view__integrity.t-num {
    font-size: var(--text-cta);
}

.wave-hud-view__bars {
    position: absolute;
    left: 0;
    top: 0;
    right: 0;
    bottom: 0;
}

.wave-hud-view__bar {
    position: absolute;
    width: 52px;
    flex-direction: column;
}

/* The elevation wrapper, in SectionCard's exact arrangement: `.elev-1` on a
 * wrapper that is padding plus the nine-sliced shadow, with the opaque
 * surface as its child. Theme.uss header note 2 has why UI Toolkit leaves no
 * other option, and the negative horizontal margin 615229c added is what puts
 * the track back on the bar's own 52px rather than 6px inside it: the wrapper
 * stretches to 64, pads 6 a side, and the track comes out 52 and centred on
 * the body it labels. Measured on the capture, row y=3: the track runs
 * x=0..51 and the ring's ink x=52..55 at 12, 8, 4 and 4 levels before paper
 * at x=56 - so `BarWidth` is unchanged and the centring `Place` does is still
 * against the thing a player sees.
 *
 * THE TOP PADDING IS CANCELLED AND THE BOTTOM IS NOT, WHICH IS THE VERTICAL
 * CASE Theme.uss's "horizontal only" DECISION EXPLICITLY SCOPED AROUND. That
 * decision is about two STACKED CARDS - cancelling vertically there would put
 * one card's white edge inside the other's shadow, and there is nothing
 * stacked here. What there is instead is a position computed from the camera:
 * `Place` positions the bar ROOT, and 6px of padding above the track moves the
 * track 6px down the body it labels. Measured before this line: the fill ran
 * y=6..11 where the previous capture had it at y=0..5. The lift is the one
 * thing this view's class comment records three failed derivations of, so 6px
 * of it is not given away to a shadow ring. The BOTTOM padding stays - that is
 * where the drop actually falls, and it is also the gap to the tag.
 *
 * ONE DECLARATION, because `flex-direction: column` is Yoga's default and
 * writing it out would be this file re-deriving the layer rather than
 * composing it - which is the thing step 5 of this task is a test for. */
.wave-hud-view__elev {
    margin-top: -6px;
}

.wave-hud-view__track {
    height: 6px;
    flex-direction: row;
    background-color: var(--hud-scrim);
    border-radius: 3px;
}

/* Default is a raider: coral, the token table's danger. */
.wave-hud-view__fill {
    height: 6px;
    border-radius: 3px;
    background-color: var(--coral);
}

/* Yours. Green - the same success colour the post-wave screen uses. */
.wave-hud-view__bar.creature .wave-hud-view__fill { background-color: var(--green); }

/* Chill applied: teal, matching the Vetch/info token. Chill is the trait the
 * wave-6 defeat screen teaches, so it should be the same colour there and
 * here or the lesson does not transfer. */
.wave-hud-view__bar.chilled .wave-hud-view__fill { background-color: var(--teal); }

/* Rally: amber-bright, the apex/attention colour. */
.wave-hud-view__bar.rallied .wave-hud-view__fill { background-color: var(--amber-bright); }

/* Breaching - the one state a player must catch in peripheral vision, so it
 * is the brightest thing on the field. */
.wave-hud-view__bar.breaching .wave-hud-view__fill { background-color: var(--coral-alert); }

/* Centred under the track it belongs to, rather than left-aligned under a
 * 52px column, so a three-letter tag and a seven-letter one both sit over the
 * body they label.
 *
 * ITS SIZE IS NOT SET HERE ANY MORE. "RALLY 42" ends in a countdown, and
 * combat_engine section 8 makes Rally the player's ONLY input during a wave -
 * so that remainder is a number a decision depends on, which is bible 10.6's
 * own test for the `t-num` marker. The class is on the element in `NewBar`
 * and it already carries --text-secondary, the exact 11px this rule used to
 * write out. Same pixels, one fewer place for the floor to be lost. */
.wave-hud-view__tag {
    -unity-text-align: upper-center;
    color: var(--surface);
}
